WEBVTT

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:09.042
[engine starts, revving]

00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:21.433
[Jerry] <i>This is a 1952 VW Bug </i>
<i>in azure blue.</i>

00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:29.633
<i>It has a four-cylinder, </i>
<i>25-horsepower engine.</i>

00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:31.333
<i>It's called a split-window</i>

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.767
<i>because the rear window was made</i>
<i>in two pieces to save money.</i>

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:39.567
<i>And if, like me, you feel that true</i>
<i>humility is always in short supply,</i>

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.367
<i>this is the car for you.</i>

00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:44.567
[telephone ringing]

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:45.900
[Larry] <i>This is Larry speaking.</i>

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.633
-[Jerry] <i>Hey, man.</i>
-[Larry] <i>Hey.</i>

00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:49.616
<i>-</i>[Jerry] <i>Wanna do something?</i>
<i>-</i>[Larry] <i>Absolutely.</i>

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:50.700
[Jerry] <i>Really? You free?</i>

00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:53.067
[Larry] <i>Uh...</i> <i>Yeah, I can be free.</i>

00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:56.189
<i>-</i>[Jerry] <i>Wanna grab a quick coffee?</i>
<i>-</i>[Larry] <i>Let's get some coffee.</i>

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.867
[Jerry] <i>Hi, I'm Jerry Seinfeld,</i>

00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:04.000
<i>and this is</i>
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

00:01:05.633 --> 00:01:07.367
<i>Today my guest is Larry David.</i>

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:09.533
<i>Larry and I have been friends</i>
<i>for over 30 years.</i>

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.733
<i>And we did a TV series</i>
<i>together in the '90s.</i>

00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:15.633
What the hell, man?

00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:19.233
[Jerry]
Do you still rely on baseball as a...

00:01:19.346 --> 00:01:20.447
What's the word, uh...

00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:22.667
Is it a palliative?
Can I use that word?

00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:24.667
Is baseball a palliative in your life?

00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:26.267
By the way, fantastic word.

00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:29.433
I've never heard it come out
of a human's mouth in my life.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.633
[upbeat music playing]

00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:36.733
I always loved this car.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.067
-Really?
-Always.

00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:39.267
You excited?

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.667
I wouldn't say I'm excited,
but I'm looking forward to it.

00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:46.233
My mother used to say to me once a week,

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.900
"Larry, why can't you get excited
about something?

00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:51.333
You're never excited.
Nothing excites you.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.933
We're going to the Irvington Hotel
in New Jersey.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.700
They have ice skating there."

00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:57.933
"No, I'm not excited."

00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:00.567
You know what's one of your
great theories?

00:02:00.633 --> 00:02:03.367
-Each generation gets better looking.
-Yes, yes.

00:02:03.433 --> 00:02:07.400
Yeah, that's really good,
observant and original thinking.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.367
It's a miracle we ever got any work done

00:02:11.433 --> 00:02:13.933
because nobody can waste time
like you and me.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.700
-Well, it is--
-Just waste it.

00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:19.267
-It is a miracle, I agree.
-It's a miracle.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:24.111
[Jerry] One of the things that the 
comedian mind-set requires is laziness.

00:02:24.333 --> 00:02:25.167
[Larry] Absolutely.

00:02:25.233 --> 00:02:27.600
-You don't wanna do real work.
-No, no.

00:02:28.133 --> 00:02:32.300
I was always... I always wanted the show
to get canceled, so I didn't have to work.

00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:33.467
[upbeat music playing]

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.433
How about this experience?

00:02:40.761 --> 00:02:42.029
You're having a different experience.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.367
I am having a different experience.
That's for sure.

00:02:49.967 --> 00:02:51.833
[Larry] I remember the thing
about this car,

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.000
everybody said,
"You can't get in an accident with it."

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:55.267
[Jerry] I'll avoid that.

00:02:55.333 --> 00:02:58.367
-Everybody in college had this car.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.767
[Jerry] But how about that view down
to the road?

00:03:00.833 --> 00:03:01.833
That's what I love.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.833
-[Jerry] You see the road so well.
-[Larry] You do.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.767
I don't like the, uh...
I don't like driving a stick.

00:03:07.133 --> 00:03:08.600
That was very Jewish.

00:03:08.967 --> 00:03:10.200
Well, I'm Jewish.

00:03:11.067 --> 00:03:14.233
And you know what?
I could be described as very Jewish.

00:03:15.433 --> 00:03:17.300
[Jerry] Gotta turn on the wipers.
Watch this.

00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:18.300
[Larry] Yeah.

00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:23.000
-[Larry] Holy cow.
-[Jerry] Isn't that hilarious?

00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:29.433
[Larry] You have no interest in driving
a '52 Hudson or something like that?

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.200
[Jerry] No, too big.
I like cars that feel like toys.

00:03:32.467 --> 00:03:34.533
-I was looking to buy one.
-Really?

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:35.800
-Yeah.
-When was this?

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.467
-This was like five years ago. Yeah.
-Five years ago.

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:40.253
-Knew it was five years ago.
-Yeah. [snorts]

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.723
-It's always five years ago.
-It's always five years ago.

00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:50.400
The lunch at a normal American restaurant
is very problematic for me.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:55.367
-Piece of fish? Salad?
-I don't like to have hot food for lunch.

00:03:55.733 --> 00:03:58.033
-Yeah, a salad, but...
-Yeah.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.500
-There's not much in the salad.
-What do you love?

00:04:00.867 --> 00:04:01.867
-For lunch?
-Yeah.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.600
I'll usually have a salad for lunch.

00:04:07.233 --> 00:04:09.000
-But then I have a protein shake.
-Wow.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.333
And where do you get that?

00:04:12.967 --> 00:04:15.133
Get it in a store.

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:16.933
Yeah.

00:04:19.967 --> 00:04:22.533
The answer was equally
as stupid as the question.

00:04:22.933 --> 00:04:25.167
[Jerry] I don't understand
that there's nothing you can eat.

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:26.700
[Larry] People are disturbed by it.

00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:27.867
My ex-wife hated it.

00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:30.700
I think that's one of the reasons
our marriage ended.

00:04:30.867 --> 00:04:32.267
She couldn't take the way you eat?

00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:34.133
You know,
because I stopped drinking coffee.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:35.233
Right.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.333
And she hated it.
I said, "What do you care?"

00:04:38.467 --> 00:04:40.300
-I had tea in the cup.
-Yeah.

00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:42.867
She said, "We can't even share
a coffee in the morning anymore."

00:04:42.933 --> 00:04:44.767
I said, "But there's something in my cup.

00:04:45.467 --> 00:04:47.200
You can't see into my cup.

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:50.033
I'm still sipping,
there's still steam coming out of it."

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.200
-Right. I know.
-"What's the difference?"

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:53.767
I ordered soup the other day.

00:04:53.833 --> 00:04:55.300
Somebody said,
"That's all you're gonna get?"

00:04:57.467 --> 00:04:59.000
"What the hell do you care?"

00:04:59.967 --> 00:05:01.200
[both laughing]

00:05:03.403 --> 00:05:05.272
[Jerry] I always think the people
in the restaurant

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:06.733
always look like that restaurant.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.133
Whatever the style of the restaurant is,

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:12.433
the people look like that style,
that's why they go there.

00:05:12.567 --> 00:05:15.500
Any restaurant you go to,
if the people in there

00:05:15.633 --> 00:05:19.300
are dressing in these muted tones
like you like...

00:05:19.533 --> 00:05:21.900
taupes, bones...

00:05:22.489 --> 00:05:24.224
-beiges...
-I, I don't...

00:05:24.291 --> 00:05:25.358
...soft grays.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.067
-I don't think so.
-No?

00:05:29.433 --> 00:05:31.467
I've evolved, clothing wise...

00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:33.166
-You have?
-...since the days I think.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.933
I know you're still with
the very soft shoulder.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.300
-You don't like a constructive shoulder.
-No. Never liked that shoulder.

00:05:42.433 --> 00:05:44.700
Yeah. It is amazing
how we got any work done.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:45.833
I agree.

00:05:50.267 --> 00:05:52.467
[Jerry] Okay, I wanna stop
in front of this car here.

00:05:52.567 --> 00:05:54.700
This is a Bugatti. That's a bee car.

00:05:54.833 --> 00:05:56.467
That's-- A bee would drive that car.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:58.533
And, you know, VW makes Bugattis.

00:05:58.733 --> 00:06:02.767
So these two cars were made
by the same company at different times.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:04.033
Wow.

00:06:04.633 --> 00:06:08.633
[Jerry] By the way, this Bugatti
has 10 radiators, my car doesn't have any.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:12.906
All right, let's go eat.

00:06:19.933 --> 00:06:21.400
But look at the turn signal.

00:06:22.633 --> 00:06:25.233
-Holy mackerel!
-[Jerry] It's called a semaphore.

00:06:25.567 --> 00:06:28.667
[Larry] I thought a semaphore
was some grammatical notation.

00:06:28.733 --> 00:06:31.200
-That's a semicolon.
-I'm gonna look that up.

00:06:34.633 --> 00:06:35.833
[Larry] You just made the distance

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:37.933
that was acceptable
for me to hold that door.

00:06:38.067 --> 00:06:39.000
[Jerry] Thank you.

00:06:39.533 --> 00:06:40.667
[upbeat music playing]

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.333
-I would like a cup of coffee.
-I'll have an herbal tea.

00:06:59.433 --> 00:07:02.200
Do you remember those
bowls of cereal I used to eat?

00:07:02.267 --> 00:07:03.900
-You still do that?
-No.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:05.300
When did this happen?

00:07:05.759 --> 00:07:07.694
-Five years ago?
-Some years ago. Five...

00:07:13.633 --> 00:07:17.300
Hey, look. I've got it in a cup.
You don't know what it is.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:18.833
Well, bully for you.

00:07:18.933 --> 00:07:21.500
And so if this is tea rather than coffee,

00:07:21.833 --> 00:07:23.867
a person should find that so disturbing?

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:27.633
I'm sorry that you're not gonna like
what I'm about to say,

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:31.567
but I'm afraid your wife
has a bit of a point.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:33.133
-Really?
-Yes.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:36.633
There's a point? Look, I can talk
just as well holding this cup

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:38.733
as if there were coffee in it.
What's the difference?

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:40.733
-Wanna know the difference?
-I do.

00:07:41.067 --> 00:07:43.700
We go to an ice cream shop, I get a cone.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.700
And you get a salad.

00:07:47.733 --> 00:07:48.733
That's the difference.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.333
And you go,
"What? I'm eating, you're eating."

00:07:53.967 --> 00:07:55.233
It's the mood.

00:08:02.633 --> 00:08:06.333
Who could say what creates mood?
Mood is a thing that's just there.

00:08:06.633 --> 00:08:08.767
The only reason we know it's there
is that we feel it.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:12.533
You're giving me a scientific argument
that I can't combat.

00:08:15.333 --> 00:08:17.833
-What's funny about that?
-I don't know.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.533
[Jerry] What is the situation
that you're in

00:08:24.633 --> 00:08:28.000
where you just start eating things
that you would not normally eat?

00:08:28.067 --> 00:08:30.200
I'll be at a dinner party, okay?

00:08:30.433 --> 00:08:33.800
They're serving, let's say, chicken, okay?

00:08:34.067 --> 00:08:37.000
-Ordinarily, I won't eat chicken.
-What the hell is wrong with chicken?

00:08:37.333 --> 00:08:38.700
It's a lot of cholesterol.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:40.733
-Chicken?
-Yeah.

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:43.133
And if it's not free-range chicken,
there's a lot wrong with it.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:46.033
How much do you think
these chickens really enjoy that range?

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:48.067
You think they're going out there
like a palomino?

00:08:48.133 --> 00:08:50.700
I think they're enjoying it a lot more
than that little cage

00:08:50.767 --> 00:08:52.133
where they're [beep] on each other.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:53.833
-A little scary.
-It's not like a range,

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:56.000
like Monument Valley, John Wayne.

00:08:56.067 --> 00:08:58.333
They're not out on the range,
you know, just...

00:08:59.267 --> 00:09:01.433
"What do you say
we can head out onto the range?"

00:09:02.267 --> 00:09:03.833
-You're looking at it...
-Yeah.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:05.633
-That kind of range.
-Yeah.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:08.433
-"Home on the Range." That range.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:09:08.533 --> 00:09:11.067
Most people would say, dietarily...

00:09:11.333 --> 00:09:13.567
-you're a master.
-I'm not a master.

00:09:13.867 --> 00:09:16.533
-No?
-No. I have snacking problems.

00:09:16.733 --> 00:09:17.633
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:20.100
-Give me the circumstances--
-When do I go off?

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:21.200
When you go off.

00:09:21.433 --> 00:09:24.767
Give me "Larry David in the debauched...

00:09:25.033 --> 00:09:26.600
-The debauched..."
-[sputters]

00:09:36.733 --> 00:09:39.167
-Oh, Jesus.
-Why do you love that word?

00:09:39.533 --> 00:09:41.633
-I don't know. It's a funny word.
-It's a funny word.

00:09:41.833 --> 00:09:44.133
What's that guy's name?
What's the <i>Penthouse</i> guy?

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:46.900
-Bob Guccione.
-It's a Bob Guccione word.

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:50.600
"You know, a lot of people think
there's just debauchery going..."

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:55.200
A real spit-take is not often.
You don't get that often, right?

00:09:55.267 --> 00:09:57.033
-Probably five years since I've seen one.
-Yeah.

00:09:57.267 --> 00:09:59.367
All right, so when am I
gonna let my guard down?

00:09:59.533 --> 00:10:01.100
When am I gonna eat like a human?

00:10:01.167 --> 00:10:02.533
When do you go out of control?

00:10:02.667 --> 00:10:04.567
Out of control is two pieces of pizza.

00:10:04.733 --> 00:10:05.933
-Right?
-Right.

00:10:06.233 --> 00:10:07.133
Never two.

00:10:09.467 --> 00:10:10.467
-Never two?
-No.

00:10:10.533 --> 00:10:11.967
I'll have six if I want.

00:10:12.033 --> 00:10:14.267
When I decide to go, when I pull that pin

00:10:14.533 --> 00:10:15.900
and I throw that grenade...

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:19.800
Me out of control,
it's half a bag of raw cashews.

00:10:20.833 --> 00:10:21.867
Oh, boy.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:26.667
I have this pitch that I've never done

00:10:26.733 --> 00:10:29.100
about what my social life was like
before <i>Seinfeld,</i>

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:31.367
and something about how, um...

00:10:32.067 --> 00:10:35.567
I was very desperate,
and yet I was also very particular.

00:10:35.633 --> 00:10:36.733
You know...

00:10:37.467 --> 00:10:39.833
which is a very odd combination.

00:10:40.067 --> 00:10:42.667
Even Quasimodo,
the Hunchback of Notre-Dame<i>,</i>

00:10:42.733 --> 00:10:46.667
he had to have the best-looking girl
in the village, you know.

00:10:47.433 --> 00:10:49.867
And his friends would try to fix him up,
they go,

00:10:49.933 --> 00:10:52.267
"No, Quasi, there's a girl in my office.

00:10:53.667 --> 00:10:55.267
I, I, I think you should meet her."

00:10:55.367 --> 00:10:56.767
"Is she good looking?"

00:10:58.333 --> 00:10:59.967
[waitress speaking indistinctly]

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:02.133
[Jerry] Want any of this?
Look how beautiful that is.

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:03.333
All right, I'll taste it.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:04.233
Great.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:07.300
[Jerry] I cannot wait to see the look
on your face when you eat this pancake.

00:11:07.633 --> 00:11:09.067
Here, put a little syrup on there.

00:11:09.667 --> 00:11:11.567
This is gonna be great.
I can't wait for this.

00:11:11.633 --> 00:11:15.100
Oh, am I excited now.
Larry David is gonna eat a pancake.

00:11:29.267 --> 00:11:32.800
I can't believe this is how people live,
that people can do this all the time.

00:11:32.933 --> 00:11:33.767
Yes.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.267
[upbeat music playing]

00:11:46.867 --> 00:11:49.833
But don't you think once it cools off,
it loses its allure?

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:51.033
The pancake?

00:11:51.233 --> 00:11:53.900
Don't you think the heat
is like a big part of it?

00:11:54.167 --> 00:11:56.033
-Yeah.
-I'm kind of intrigued by that.

00:11:56.367 --> 00:11:58.067
Heat doesn't have any flavor.

00:11:58.233 --> 00:12:01.000
-It's inviting.
-Why? What does it do?

00:12:01.233 --> 00:12:02.700
It warms you.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.500
That's very Kramer-ish.

00:12:04.567 --> 00:12:08.833
"It's good, Jerry. Heat, it warms you."

00:12:09.933 --> 00:12:13.333
When I think back on the show,
you would pitch me some premise,

00:12:13.433 --> 00:12:17.400
some insane, absurd thing,
and I would just go, "Okay."

00:12:18.233 --> 00:12:20.500
-Right. Yeah.
-And you would just crack up.

00:12:20.667 --> 00:12:21.500
Okay, yeah.

00:12:21.567 --> 00:12:23.567
'Cause it's something
I won't even tell another soul.

00:12:23.733 --> 00:12:25.333
And now we're gonna do it on TV.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:27.567
You would have a cigar after lunch,
remember that?

00:12:27.733 --> 00:12:29.067
-Right in the office, right?
-Yes.

00:12:29.367 --> 00:12:31.233
And you would offer sage advice.

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:36.800
You seemed like, "Yes, come, my child.
I've got this all figured out."

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:39.967
Anything you say with a cigar
just sounds so much better.

00:12:40.067 --> 00:12:42.367
It imbues you with knowledge.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:43.800
What is it about it?

00:12:44.433 --> 00:12:46.267
There's something contemplative about it.

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:49.800
-Why not a cigarette?
-A cigarette isn't contemplative.

00:12:50.267 --> 00:12:51.800
It's not that...

00:12:51.867 --> 00:12:54.200
A cigarette... Is that.

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:55.767
-Right.
-It's anxiety.

00:12:55.833 --> 00:12:58.100
There's anxiety associated with it.

00:12:58.533 --> 00:12:59.733
-You know what I mean?
-Right.

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:01.700
Whereas the cigar is relaxing.

00:13:01.767 --> 00:13:05.767
And when you relax,
you're open to more thoughts.

00:13:05.833 --> 00:13:07.367
You have time to think.

00:13:07.433 --> 00:13:09.100
A cigar takes time.

00:13:09.167 --> 00:13:11.300
-A cigar takes time.
-That's the key to it.

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:13.567
That's the whole key.

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:16.400
Someone smoking a cigar is in no hurry.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.167
-Absolutely, you're in no hurry at all.
-Right.

00:13:19.367 --> 00:13:22.200
-Whereas the cigarette smoker, rushed.
-Yeah.

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:24.600
Gotta get out, you know.

00:13:24.833 --> 00:13:27.733
-But a cigarette takes some time.
-Yes, I know, but...

00:13:30.667 --> 00:13:32.567
A cigarette is just, it's just...

00:13:32.933 --> 00:13:34.400
It's not something you're enjoying.

00:13:34.467 --> 00:13:40.000
Oh, right. You continue to have
one of the finest minds I've ever met.

00:13:40.067 --> 00:13:41.967
Coming from you,
I take that as a great compliment.

00:13:42.033 --> 00:13:45.200
From day one,
"This guy's got some head on him."

00:13:45.333 --> 00:13:48.100
That cigar-cigarette thing, I love that.

00:13:48.533 --> 00:13:51.200
Here's an analysis of something
by two idiots

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:53.667
that no one else is doing.

00:13:53.733 --> 00:13:56.800
You have finally done
the show about nothing.

00:13:58.004 --> 00:13:59.772
-Here's your change. Thank you.
-Thank you.

00:13:59.867 --> 00:14:01.667
Okay, it's $41.

00:14:02.133 --> 00:14:03.100
What's your tip?

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:04.967
What's, what's your tip policy in general?

00:14:05.033 --> 00:14:05.900
Exorbitant.

00:14:06.012 --> 00:14:08.514
And if I may say, tip anxiety.

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:10.600
I think it's a Jewish thing.

00:14:10.667 --> 00:14:12.567
I used to do that bit on that.

00:14:12.767 --> 00:14:15.100
Representing the tribe, in a way.

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:19.367
And, and, and it's...

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:22.267
You need to leave decent amounts
so they don't think you're a cheap Jim.

00:14:22.333 --> 00:14:24.667
Then, you know, you can't give too much...

00:14:25.264 --> 00:14:27.834
'cause then you don't want them to think
you're a showy Jim.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:31.367
There's that, too.
You gotta straddle the line.

00:14:32.033 --> 00:14:34.300
'Cause they'll get you on
either end of this.

00:14:34.567 --> 00:14:36.900
You know, you come in with a cigar...

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:39.433
